{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "Conventionally, these surgeries are planned using 2-dimensional (2D) imaging modalities, such as computed tomography (CT) and X-ray imaging [10]. Such 2D renderings, however, fail to accurately depict the structural complexities of bony fractures, which may lead to imperfect reconstruction. This is problematic in the context of foot and ankle fractures, as improper reduction can lead to post-traumatic osteoarthritis of articular surfaces, which in turn can greatly impair mobility [4, 10]. 3D printing, also known as additive manufacturing or rapid prototyping, is a rapidly expanding technology that is beginning to revolutionise the medical and healthcare industry [11, 12].",
      "reason": "The introduction describes the current state of knowledge regarding conventional 2D planning and its limitations, as well as the emergence of 3D printing technology."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "In this context, the 3D printed models facilitate surgery by enabling an enhanced understanding of the anatomy, improved communication between clinicians and patients, and improved tailoring of both tools and surgical techniques to the individual fracture [19]; as such, this new approach constitutes a form of personalised medicine, which is central to contemporary healthcare.",
      "reason": "The text explains the potential benefits of 3D printing in surgical planning, establishing the rationale for investigating whether this technology improves outcomes."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The manuscript does not mention the existence of previous systematic reviews on this specific topic, so this conditional requirement does not apply."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "Briefly, the 3D printing process consists of three stages: image acquisition, image processing and 3D printing. The first step involves using imaging modalities such as CT images to obtain an image of the fracture site. These are then stored in a digital imaging and communications in medicine (DICOM) format. Then, image processing entails the conversion of the DICOM images to standard triangulation language (STL) files using software packages, whereby the images are segmented to create a triangular mesh. From these, the 3D model is printed [16, 19].",
      "reason": "The review describes the mechanism of action (the 3D printing process) for the intervention being studied."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The intervention is not described as a complex intervention requiring a logic model in the context of this review."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 100
}

